---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/rock-cycle">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson introduces the machine that recycles the planet's outer skin: the
    <strong>rock cycle</strong>. You need only the pieces picked up along the way — minerals
    (the ingredients), plate tectonics (the conveyor), deep time (the schedule) — and all
    three are rebuilt in place as they are needed. Everything else is built here, step by
    step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the three rock families and the process that manufactures each — freezing, assembling, transforming;</li>
    <li>trace any material through the cycle by any route, including the shortcuts that skip stages;</li>
    <li>explain the two engines that power the loop — Earth's internal heat and the Sun-driven water cycle — and set its pace;</li>
    <li>read cooling history from igneous texture, environment from sedimentary layers, and pressure history from metamorphic fabrics;</li>
    <li>explain metamorphism as solid-state cooking, and foliation as a fossilised compass;</li>
    <li>run the field routine that sorts any hand sample into its first box.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the page: every rock is a <em>stage</em>, not a species — the
    same atoms pass through all three families, over and over, and the rock in your hand is
    a photograph of one moment of their journey.
  </p>

  <h2>Rocks are temporary forms</h2>
  <p>
    Rocks are not forever — they are <em>temporary forms</em> in a planetary recycling loop.
    The stone of a future mountain is, right now, probably seafloor mud; the pebbles in your
    garden path were once the inside of a volcano; rocks deep in the crust are becoming other
    kinds of rock as you read. Geology's great insight, won slowly against scientific
    resistance, is <strong>deep time</strong>: James Hutton, standing at Siccar Point on the
    Scottish coast in 1788, read a vanished mountain range buried inside seafloor layers
    tilted and eroded before more layers were laid on top — and realised the Earth's history
    needed not thousands of years but an almost inconceivable span, with "no vestige of a
    beginning, no prospect of an end". The same material can be igneous, sedimentary and
    metamorphic, over and over, across billions of years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three great classes mark the three states of the loop: <strong>igneous</strong> rock is
    frozen magma; <strong>sedimentary</strong> rock is assembled debris;
    <strong>metamorphic</strong> rock is older rock transformed, solid, by heat and pressure.
    Scrub through one full revolution below — at mountain-belt pace, a few tens of millions
    of years per lap.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each family with its manufacture" widget="match" config={rcFamilyMatch} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the loop as steps — scrub it like a cell dividing, but at mountain-belt pace" widget="timeline" config={rockTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step 1 doubles as step 7 — the loop has no starting
    line, only a convenient place to board. Watch the middle steps for the texture logic:
    the same magma makes coarse granite or fine basalt depending only on <em>cooling
    speed</em> (step 2), and the same debris becomes sandstone, shale or limestone depending
    on where it settles (step 4). And notice what step 5 refuses to do: melt. The moment
    rock melts, it leaves the metamorphic story and re-enters as magma — the cycle's
    strictest rule, which the next sections explain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one loop, three rock classes, no beginning and no end. But the
    stepped timeline hides something important — there is more than one route through the
    cycle — so the next section draws it as a map instead of a wheel.
  </p>

  <h2>The loop, node by node</h2>
  <p>
    The cycle is easier to navigate as a map, because there is more than one route through
    it. Click the stops below: any rock can be melted back to magma, weathered to sediment,
    or metamorphosed in place — and each of those outcomes can itself be revisited. A
    granite can erode into sand, become sandstone, then quartzite, then melt; a shale can
    skip the sediment stage entirely and transform straight to schist under a mountain
    range.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk a granite's biography" widget="branch" config={rcBiographyBranch} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="the recycling map — click any rock type to see how it leaves, and how it returns" widget="diagram" config={cycleCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the solid arrows trace the grand tour — melt, freeze,
    erode, bury, transform, melt again — but the dashed arrows are where real geology gets
    interesting. Igneous rock buried near an intrusion can metamorphose <em>directly</em>,
    with no sedimentary holiday in between; uplifted metamorphic rock can be stripped
    straight back to sediment. There is no set progression, no mandatory stations: the
    diagram is a road network, not a ferris wheel, and a rock's biography is whatever path
    the plates happened to drive it along.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the rock cycle is not a neat circle with one track. Rocks
  skip stages constantly — igneous can metamorphose directly, sedimentary can melt directly,
  and some rocks cycle many times without ever visiting another class. The diagram above is a
  road network, not a ferris wheel.</p>

  <h2>Two engines drive the wheel</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing about the rock cycle happens for free, and its power comes from two mismatched
    engines. Earth's <strong>internal heat</strong> — left over from the planet's formation
    and topped up continuously by radioactive decay — drives the deep half: melting,
    volcanism, subduction and mountain-building, all organised by plate tectonics, which
    acts as the cycle's conveyor belt (subduction drags surface rock down toward the melting
    zone; collisions uplift it back). Magma — rock melted to liquid — runs between about
    800 and 1,300 °C depending on composition and pressure, and it either cools slowly
    within the crust over centuries to millions of years or erupts and freezes in seconds to
    years. Sunlight, meanwhile, powers the shallow half: weather evaporates water, rain
    freezes and thaws, rivers run, and all of that weathers rock into grains and carries
    them to the sea. The rock cycle is therefore half geology, half solar power — a rare
    machine driven simultaneously from below and above.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="file each process by its engine" widget="sort-buckets" config={rcEngineSort} />
  <p>
    The two engines also set the pace. Surface processes (weathering, erosion, deposition)
    are comparatively quick: a river can carve a valley in a million years, and sediments
    buried in a basin can harden to rock within a few million. Deep processes are slow:
    subduction delivers rock to the melting zone over tens of millions of years, and the
    fastest regional metamorphism still takes millions — a conservative estimate for each
    major leg of the journey is around 20 million years, so one full lap runs to something
    like 60 million. The cycle turns no faster than its slowest arc — which is why, despite
    four and a half billion years of operation, the loop is nowhere near "finished".
  </p>
  <p>
    The machine is also <em>comparatively</em> alive — an accident of being Earth. The
    cycle still runs here because our core is hot enough to keep the mantle moving, our
    atmosphere is thick, and liquid water survives at the surface. On the Moon, by contrast,
    the rock cycle is virtually dead: the core has cooled past the point of driving mantle
    convection, and there is no atmosphere or water to weather anything — lunar rock simply
    waits, pulverised by impacts and radiation, for a recycling that will never come. When
    you hold a terrestrial pebble, you are holding output from a machine the Moon has lost.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the rock cycle is ancient history — the Earth made its
  rocks long ago and now they just sit there." It seems reasonable because geologic change
  is invisible at human timescales. But the cycle is running now: lava is freezing into new
  igneous crust this year, rivers are depositing sediment that will be sandstone, and
  slabs are dragging crust toward the melting zone beneath every subduction zone. The
  evidence is everywhere — young seafloor, growing deltas, active volcanoes — you only
  need the timescale to see the motion.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the route map and its power supply — a conveyor driven from
    below by the planet's own heat and from above by the Sun. Now we descend into the stops
    themselves: what each family looks like in the hand, and what its textures confess
    about how it was made.
  </p>

  <h2>The three families in the field</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Igneous rocks</strong> are hard, crystalline and unlayered, and their texture
    tells you their cooling story. Cool slowly deep underground (as granite does) and
    crystals have time to grow large enough to see; erupt as lava (basalt) and the same
    chemistry freezes so fast that crystals stay microscopic; quench instantly and you get
    obsidian, a natural glass. Giant's Causeway in Northern Ireland is basalt that shrank
    into hexagonal columns as it cooled — geometry from physics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — igneous textures" widget="spot-error" config={rcIgneousSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>Sedimentary rocks</strong> are layered, usually softer, and often
    fossil-bearing — the archive of Earth's surface history. Sandstone was once sand (a
    river bed, a beach, a desert dune); shale was compacted mud on a quiet seafloor;
    limestone is built of shells and reef debris. The white cliffs of Dover are chalk made
    of uncountable marine algae plates. Because sediments pile up from the bottom, their
    layers record time and environment — which is why sedimentary rocks carry most of the
    fossil record, and also why coal, oil and gas are found only in them: fossil fuels are
    buried organic matter, and burial means sediment.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the sedimentary archive" widget="judge" config={rcSedJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>Metamorphic rocks</strong> are the banded, streaked, sometimes sparkly ones —
    slate roofs, marble statues, gneiss shields. Their appearance is a pressure record: the
    more tortured the banding, the harder the rock was squeezed. Two field clues separate
    them from layered sedimentary rocks: metamorphic layers (foliation) are
    <em>mineral alignments</em>, wavy and often folded, while sedimentary layers are grain
    deposits, flat and clean; and metamorphic rocks resist — they are what rock becomes
    after surviving conditions that would have destroyed it once already.
  </p>

  <h2>Metamorphism: cooking without melting</h2>
  <p>
    Metamorphism changes rock <em>in the solid state</em>. Bury a shale — heat climbs with
    depth at roughly 25–30 °C per kilometre, and pressure grows with the weight above — and
    at a few kilometres down the clay minerals become unstable and rebuild into micas, all
    while the rock stays solid. Push further and new, denser minerals form in sequence. This
    is why metamorphism sits between sedimentation and melting: hot enough to rearrange
    atoms, not hot enough to melt.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the crust's heat climb" widget="estimate" config={rcGradientEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> metamorphic rock is not "partly melted rock". If the
  rock melts, it becomes magma and leaves the metamorphic club entirely — the
  metamorphic world is strictly solid-state chemistry, which is what makes its textures so
  distinctive.</p>
  <p>
    The three agents act differently. <strong>Heat</strong> drives new mineral growth;
    <strong>pressure</strong> (equal in all directions — confining pressure) compresses
    pores and recrystallises; <strong>differential stress</strong> — squeezing harder in one
    direction — aligns minerals, creating the flat fabrics below; and <strong>chemically
    active fluids</strong> ferry atoms around like solvents, speeding every reaction.
    Contact metamorphism (an intrusion baking its wall rock — marble around a granite) is
    heat-dominated and fabric-free; regional metamorphism (whole mountain belts) is the
    full squeeze. The classic upgrades: shale → slate → schist → gneiss as intensity rises;
    limestone → marble; sandstone → quartzite.
  </p>

  <h2>Foliation and lineation: the pressure record</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Foliation</strong> is any planar fabric imposed by differential stress. Its
    mechanism is simple and beautiful: platy minerals like mica, growing under a directional
    squeeze, orient themselves perpendicular to the maximum compression — like cards fanned
    flat when you press a deck between your palms. At low grades the alignment is a fine
    <em>cleavage</em> (slate splits into clean roofing sheets along it); at higher grades the
    micas grow larger and the rock becomes glittery <em>schist</em>; at the highest grades
    light and dark minerals segregate into stripes — <em>gneissic banding</em>.
    <strong>Lineation</strong> is the companion fabric: elongate minerals or stretched
    pebbles aligned like grain in wood, recording the direction of flow. A geologist can
    therefore read not just how hot and squeezed a rock was, but <em>which way</em> the
    squeeze came from — foliation is a fossilised compass.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="foliation vs lineation — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={rcFabricCompare} />
  <p>
    Not all metamorphic rocks are foliated. Marble (from limestone) and quartzite (from
    sandstone) are <em>non-foliated</em>, because their parent minerals — calcite, quartz —
    grow into interlocking equant grains rather than plates: there is nothing to align.
    Foliation needs platy or needle-like minerals, which is why mudrocks make the most
    dramatic slates and why a marble quarry wall shows no layering at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Metamorphic grade and facies</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Grade</strong> is the intensity of metamorphism, and it is read from
    <em>index minerals</em> that are stable only in certain temperature windows. Barrovian
    zones (after George Barrow, mapping Scotland in the 1890s) march from chlorite through
    biotite, garnet and staurolite to kyanite and sillimanite: find garnet, you are in
    moderate grade; find sillimanite, the rock has been near its melting ceiling. In slate
    the original clay is barely transformed; in gneiss the rock is on magma's doorstep.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="sequence the Barrovian index minerals" widget="order" config={rcBarrovianOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>Metamorphic facies</strong> generalise the idea to pressure as well as
    temperature: each facies is a pressure–temperature neighbourhood with its own mineral
    suite. Greenschist and amphibolite mark ordinary burial and mountain-belt conditions;
    granulite marks the hot lower crust; and <em>blueschist</em> — cold but crushing
    pressure — is the fingerprint of subduction zones, where cold slabs dive fast enough to
    be squeezed without being warmed. Reading a facies out of a rock is reading the tectonic
    setting it passed through: the rock cycle's map, and the plate-tectonics story,
    compressed into mineral choices.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifying a rock: the field routine</h2>
  <p>
    Geologists classify a hand sample with a routine you can run on any pebble. First ask
    <em>are there layers, and are they mineral alignments or deposited grains?</em> —
    separating metamorphic from sedimentary. Then check <em>grain size and texture</em>:
    interlocking crystals with no layering says igneous, and the crystal size says how fast
    it cooled (coarse = intrusive granite, fine = extruded basalt, glassy = quenched
    obsidian). Fossils or rounded grains cemented together say sedimentary; drop dilute
    acid on it and a fizz pins limestone (calcite) instantly; a light, soapy-feeling
    grey-white block that scratches glass is quartzite (metamorphosed sandstone); a rock
    that splits into flat roofing sheets along a silky sheen is slate. A three-question
    field test — <em>layered? crystals? fossils?</em> — plus one acid bottle sorts the
    entire rock kingdom into its first box, and every museum drawer in the world is
    organised by some version of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the cycle matters beyond geology</h2>
  <p>
    The loop is not an internal affair. Its surface half makes <strong>soil</strong> —
    rock weathered to the fine mineral grains that roots need — which makes the cycle, at
    one remove, the foundation of the food chain. Its burial half concentrates
    <strong>resources</strong>: coal, oil and gas form only in sedimentary basins, marble
    and granite are quarried as building materials precisely because the cycle produced
    them in enormous, workable volumes, and the metals of the minerals lesson ride the same
    conveyor. Knowing how the cycle routes material is knowing where to look — which is why
    the same diagram hangs in mining company offices and national geological surveys alike.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Starting from a sedimentary rock, list the legs of a journey that ends as a new sedimentary rock — and estimate the total time.</li>
    <li>How can you tell a foliated metamorphic rock from a layered sedimentary one, given that both show bands?</li>
    <li>Why is marble never foliated, though limestone's cousin shale becomes the most beautifully foliated slate?</li>
    <li>The Moon has igneous rocks. Why has it no working rock cycle?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Erode to sediment (uplift and weathering), deposit and
    bury until compacted and cemented into sedimentary rock — or take the long route:
    bury deeper to metamorphose, then melt to magma, freeze as igneous, and erode again.
    Each major leg runs roughly 20 million years, so a full lap is on the order of
    60 million years or more. (2) Metamorphic bands are <em>mineral alignments</em> — wavy,
    folded, continuous, formed by directional squeeze; sedimentary layers are deposits of
    grains — flat, clean, with texture changes between beds. Slate's sheen splits along
    aligned micas; shale splits along bedding planes of deposited mud. (3) Foliation needs
    platy minerals to align; limestone's calcite recrystallises into equant interlocking
    grains (marble) with nothing plate-like to line up — while shale's clays rebuild into
    micas, perfect for alignment. (4) The cycle needs both engines: internal heat to drive
    melting, subduction and uplift, and solar-powered weathering and water to make sediment.
    The Moon's core has cooled past driving convection, and it has no atmosphere or liquid
    water — both engines are off, so lunar rock just accumulates.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rocks are temporary forms: the same material cycles through igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic states with no beginning and no end.</li>
    <li>Igneous = frozen magma (slow cooling gives coarse crystals, fast cooling gives fine, instant quench gives glass).</li>
    <li>Sedimentary = assembled debris: weathering, transport, deposition, compaction, cementation — and it keeps the fossils and the fossil fuels.</li>
    <li>Metamorphic = transformed in the solid state by heat, pressure, differential stress and fluids; melting evicts a rock from the metamorphic club.</li>
    <li>The cycle is a road network, not a ferris wheel: rocks skip stages, and shortcuts (igneous → metamorphic, uplift → erosion) are routine.</li>
    <li>Two engines power it: Earth's internal heat (magma runs 800–1,300 °C) through plate tectonics, and sunlight through the water cycle.</li>
    <li>A lap takes tens of millions of years — about 20 million per major leg is a fair estimate.</li>
    <li>Foliation is mica aligned perpendicular to the squeeze: a fossilised compass; grade is read from index minerals, setting from facies (blueschist = subduction).</li>
    <li>Field routine: layered? crystals? fossils? — plus an acid bottle — sorts any hand sample.</li>
    <li>The cycle needs both engines: on airless, cooled worlds like the Moon, it is dead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The cycle drawn here is the syllabus's spine for the next three lessons. Volcanoes and
    igneous processes open the melting arc — what magma actually does on its way up.
    Weathering, erosion and landforms open the surface arc — how rock becomes grain and
    grain becomes landscape. And fossils and geologic time give the loop its clock, so the
    "few tens of millions of years per lap" of this page becomes a dated story. Every later
    unit — deformation, earthquakes, even climate — rides some arc of this wheel, so keep
    the map handy.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Deep time has a paper trail. Hutton's Siccar Point "unconformity" — tilted red sandstone
    beneath horizontal grey one, with the missing eroded interval spanning unknown millions
    of years — became the founding argument for uniformitarianism: the idea that today's
    processes explain the past ("the present is the key to the past"). The oldest surviving
    crustal rocks are metamorphic survivors: the Acasta gneiss of northwest Canada at about
    4.03 billion years, with older (though disputed) claims from the Nuvvuagittuq belt; and
    the single oldest known Earth material is a zircon crystal from the Jack Hills of
    Australia dated to about 4.4 billion years — a grain that outlived every rock around it
    because zircons are almost indestructible and carry uranium, nature's own clock, inside
    them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Modern metamorphic petrology is quantitative: from the minerals present and their
    compositions, thermodynamic models reconstruct the exact pressure–temperature–time path
    a rock followed — up to 800 °C and 15,000 atmospheres and back. The paths reveal
    deep-earth mechanics: rocks buried at 60–80 km in subduction zones (eclogite facies)
    that resurfaced intact, a process called exhumation that geologists are still arguing
    over; metamorphic "core complexes" where the deep crust rises like a raisin in rising
    dough; and fluids released from descending plates that trigger melting above them — the
    reason the Pacific's "Ring of Fire" exists. Every one of those stories was read out of
    solid rock, using the fabrics and facies this lesson introduced: geology's version of
    forensics, where the evidence has been sitting under a mountain range for fifty million
    years.
  </p>

